hadiths[49]{hadithnumber,text}:
"7201","6132 - And we found Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to us, he said: I was with Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi, and a man asked him: O Abu Hamzah, what do you say about approaching women in their rears? He turned away or was silent, and said: This is a shaykh of Quraysh, ask him — meaning Abdullah ibn Ali ibn al-Sa'ib. Abdullah said: O Allah, filth; had it been lawful — he said: he would have narrated to me, but he had heard nothing therein. He said: Then Abdullah ibn Ali informed me that he met Amr ibn Uhayhah ibn al-Julah and asked him about that. He said: I bear witness I heard Khuzaymah ibn Thabit — whose testimony the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made as two men's — say: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I come to my wife from her rear. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes — said it twice or thrice. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) realized and said: In which of the two holes, or which of the two piercings, or which of the two seams? As for from her rear in her front passage, yes; as for in her rear passage, Allah the Exalted forbids you to come to women in their rears."
"7202","6133 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Harith ibn Mukhallad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah Almighty will not look at a man who has intercourse with his wife in her rear."
"7203","6134 - And we found Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: It is the lesser sodomy — meaning intercourse with women in their rears. And we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, Ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Amr — without raising it — who said about one who comes to a woman in her rear: The lesser sodomy. In this chapter are other reports on prohibiting this meaning we left, since in their chains is what prevents acceptance. Then we returned to interpreting Allah's saying: Your women are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage as you will. We found tillage is sought for offspring, and offspring exists in intercourse in the front passage and not in intercourse elsewhere, indicating the intent is what is permitted thereof from which offspring comes, not what yields none. Thus all Kufan jurists held in this chapter."
"7204","995 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding killing cloister-dwellers. 6135 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he dispatched armies, would say: Go out in Allah's name; fight whoever disbelieves in Allah; do not betray, nor mutilate, nor steal spoils, nor kill children, nor cloister-dwellers. Abu Ja'far said: We know no other hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on forbidding killing cloister-dwellers besides this, and it revolves upon Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Habibah al-Ashhali. And it was narrated from Abu Bakr what agrees with this meaning."
"7205","As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me: that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq — may Allah be pleased with him — when he sent troops toward Syria — Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, Amr ibn al-As and Shurahbil ibn Hasanah — among his bequests was: do not kill children, nor old men, nor women. And he said: You will find people who confined themselves to cloisters, so leave them and what they confined themselves to; and you will find others in whose heads Satan made nests, so when you find those, strike their necks, Allah willing. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates this meaning."
"7206","6136 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Dhakwan, from Muraqqi' ibn Sayfi, from Hanzalah the scribe who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we passed a woman of fine stature with people gathered over her; when he came they parted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She would not have been fighting. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent word to Khalid: not to kill a woman nor a hireling. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith reverts to Hanzalah the scribe, and we know no one who followed al-Thawri in narrating it thus. Among those who opposed him therein was al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hizami."
"7207","6137 - As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad: Muraqqi' ibn Sayfi narrated to me: my grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi', brother of Hanzalah the scribe, informed me: that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a raid, with Khalid ibn al-Walid at the vanguard. Rabah and the Companions passed a slain woman struck by the vanguard, standing over her looking in wonder at her stature until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) caught up on his camel. They parted from the woman, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped then said: She would not have been fighting. Then he looked at the people's faces and said to one of them: Catch up with Khalid ibn al-Walid and tell him: Do not kill offspring nor a hireling. And among them Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from his father."
"7208","6138 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father who said: al-Muraqqi' ibn Sayfi narrated to me: that his grandfather Rabah ibn al-Rabi', brother of Hanzalah the scribe, informed him: that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a raid he raided, then he mentioned the like of it. Yunus said: Rabah ibn al-Rabi', not al-Rabi' ibn Rabah. In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's saying about the woman: She would not have been fighting. Other than fighting Muslims may be of fighting — planning in war and inciting to fight — so whoever is so, his killing is lawful, man or woman. In what we mentioned is evidence of this meaning."
"7209","996 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever wrongfully takes a span of land will be collared with it from seven earths. 6139 - Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever wrongfully takes anything of land will be collared with it from seven earths, and whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr. Sufyan was opposed in this hadith's chain, inserting between Talhah and Sa'id Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Sahl. Among those who narrated it from al-Zuhri thus was Malik ibn Anas."
"7210","6140 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Sahl, from Sa'id ibn Zayd who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wrongfully takes anything of land will be collared with it from seven earths. And among them Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar."
"7211","6141 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Ubaydullah al-Kurayzi narrated to us, Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Sahl, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever steals anything of land will be collared with it from seven earths."
"7212","6142 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah, from Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id the like of it. Except Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali opposed Ahmad ibn Salih in this hadith's chain, not mentioning Abd al-Rahman ibn Sahl therein."
"7213","6143 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah ibn Awf, from Sa'id ibn Zayd that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wrongfully takes a span of land, Allah will collar him with it from seven earths. Sulayman ibn Kathir agreed with Sufyan in omitting Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr, narrator of this hadith's chain."
"7214","6144 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi and Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdullah, from Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Nufayl: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wrongfully takes anything of land will be collared with it from seven earths, and whoever is killed defending his wealth is a martyr."
"7215","6145 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah who said: I disputed over land, so Aishah said to me: O Abu Salamah, avoid the land; for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wrongfully takes a span of land will be collared with it from seven earths."
"7216","6146 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever wrongfully takes a span of land will be collared with it from seven earths. We reflected on the Prophet's saying, will be collared from seven earths: it may be, in wronging people, if Allah's subtlety is hidden, that He makes it what He wills of what He gives a soul to, then collars that wrongdoer therewith as torment, as narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is done on Resurrection Day to one who withheld its zakah in the world."
"7217","6147 - As al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Jami' ibn Abi Rashid and Abd al-Malik ibn A'yan narrated to us: they heard Abu Wa'il inform from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud who says: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no man who does not pay his wealth's zakah but a bald serpent is made for him on Resurrection Day; he flees from it and it follows him until his neck is collared with it. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to us: They will be collared with what they were stingy with on Resurrection Day. So Allah Almighty by His subtlety may return the wronged land in the Hereafter to like what He returns the zakah-withheld wealth to, until collaring therewith whoever wronged it in the world."
"7218","997 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the punishment of one who takes a span of land in the world — how it is on Resurrection Day — seemingly contrary to the first chapter. 6148 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Whoever takes a span of land will be sunk with it to seven earths."
"7219","6149 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Isma'il — meaning Ibn Abi Khalid — from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Thabit Ayman who said: Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wrongfully takes a span of land will come carrying it on his neck on Resurrection Day."
"7220","6150 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Abu Ya'fur narrated to us, Abu Thabit narrated to us, he said: I heard Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever takes land without its right will be charged to carry its soil to the gathering place."
"7221","6151 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Isma'il al-Attar al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ya'fur: Abu Thabit narrated to us, he said: I heard Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi mention from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. An objector said: What you narrated in this chapter contradicts what you narrated in the previous chapter. Our answer: There is no contradiction in any of that; rather these are Allah's punishments for one who wrongs a span of land, as in these reports."
"7222","998 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying about pledge: The back is ridden for its maintenance when pledged, and the milk of the milch animal is drunk for its maintenance when pledged. 6152 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The back is ridden for its maintenance when pledged, and the milk of the milch animal is drunk for its maintenance when pledged. It was not clarified in this hadith who is meant by riding the back and drinking the milk, and some people — al-Shafi'i — took it as the pledgor. Others of scholars took it contrarily, so we examined whether its clarification was narrated in any hadith."
"7223","6153 - So we found Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Hurayrah: he mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the animal is pledged, its fodder is upon the pledgee, and the milk of the milch is drunk, with its maintenance and riding upon the drinker. This hadith clarified the one meant by riding and drinking milk — the pledgee, not the pledgor. This, in our view — and Allah knows best — since all scholars oppose it despite its narrators' uprightness, is abrogated; for they are trusted in what they acted as in what they narrated, for otherwise their uprightness falls and their narration falls. Among what indicates that, and that abrogation befell this hadith: Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi who said: Nothing of the pledge is benefited from. This al-Sha'bi, upon whom this hadith turns, said what we narrated from him in the first hadith, indicating he said it only after its abrogation was established with him. Since Allah described pledge in His Book saying: Pledges held, that indicates the held is what the pledgee's hand falls upon with the pledgor's hand removed, sufficing herein. Among those forbidding that — giving neither pledgor nor pledgee benefit — were Hijazi and Iraqi jurists. And success is from Allah."
"7224","999 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his answering one who asked about Islam, does it have an end? 6154 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Kurz ibn Alqamah: that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, does Islam have an end? He said: Yes. There will be a household of Arabs or non-Arabs, when Allah wills good for them He brings Islam to them. He said: Then what? He said: Then tribulations fall like shades. A man said: No, Allah willing. He said: You will return therein as black serpents pouring forth, striking each other's necks. Al-Zuhri said: Al-aswad is the black snake: when it wants to bite, it rises then pours down. An objector said: You narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what repels this meaning, and mentioned."
"7225","6155 - What Fahd and Ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Safwan ibn Amr informed us, from Sulaym ibn Amir al-Kala'i, from Tamim al-Dari who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: This matter will reach what night reaches, and Allah will leave no house of mud nor hair but Allah will bring this religion into it, with the might of one mighty by whom Islam is honored, and the humiliation of one humbled by whom disbelief is humbled. He said: This indicates it does not cease until Allah fills the earth without interruption. Our answer: The intent in Tamim's hadith may be the whole earth, until no house remains but entered either with the might or humiliation mentioned, while the end in Kurz ibn Alqamah's hadith is the end with the people who act upon it, enter it and are its people; then tribulations come preoccupying whom Allah wills from holding to Islam. So Tamim's hadith is upon its generality equally, and Kurz's upon its cessation from some people by preoccupation with tribulation after entering among those it encompassed, for it was in the land night reaches. This is the best we have in interpreting these two hadiths, reconciling them and negating contradiction — and Allah knows best the reality — and success is from Allah."
"7226","1000 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about livestock spoiling crops by night and by day. 6156 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya ibn Dinar informed us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah and Abdullah ibn Isa, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Muhayyisah, from al-Bara: that a camel of al-Bara's family spoiled something, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled: guarding produce is upon its people by day, and made livestock people liable for what their livestock spoiled by night."
"7227","6157 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Awza'i who said: al-Zuhri informed me, from Haram ibn Muhayyisah al-Ansari that he informed him: that al-Bara ibn Azib had a vicious camel that entered a garden and spoiled therein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was spoken to about it, and ruled: guarding gardens is upon their people by day, guarding livestock upon their people by night, and upon livestock people is what it hits by night."
"7228","6158 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from Haram, then he mentioned the like of it. In the two narrations of Shu'ayb and Baqiyyah from al-Awza'i is what indicates no confirmation of Haram taking it from al-Bara, for he said anna, and the difference between an and anna in hadith is that an is upon hearing until otherwise known, and anna upon disconnection until otherwise known. Since al-Awza'i's hadith contradicted as mentioned, neither of these two hadiths and meanings is worthier than the other therein. Then we returned to the narration of the most reliable in al-Zuhri, peerless therein, to see their narration from him."
"7229","6159 - So we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhri, from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah: that a camel of al-Bara entered a man's garden and spoiled therein, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"7230","6160 - And we found al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah: that a camel of al-Bara entered a man's garden and spoiled therein, then he mentioned the rest. So the hadith returns to disconnection as Malik and Ibn Uyaynah narrated it from al-Zuhri. What Abdullah ibn Isa and Isma'il ibn Umayyah narrated from him, though eminent, must not oppose what the authority in al-Zuhri narrated contrary to them. Then we examined this hadith: we found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And upon livestock people is what their livestock spoiled by night — indicating liability for what it hits by night of crops, humans and others; for whoever must guard a thing is liable for what leaves his guard to offend. We found all scholars agree no liability upon them for what it hits by night of humans, so the hadith's apparent contradicts that. So we understood this hadith was abrogated by the Prophet's saying: The trampling animal's offense is void. What is void is wasted. Thus Abu Hanifah and companions say about livestock damage; Hijazis hold the first view. And Allah the Exalted is the Granter of success."
"7231","1001 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding sale of fresh dates for dry dates. 6161 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik ibn Anas and Usamah ibn Zayd informed him, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, that Zayd Abu Ayyash informed him: that he asked Sa'd about sult for white grain. Sa'd said: I witnessed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and he said: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. He said: Then no, and disliked it. As for Malik's hadith from this Abdullah ibn Yazid, there is no disagreement from him therein as we narrated from him."
"7232","6162 - And al-Muzani also narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, that Zayd Abu Ayyash informed him that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas was asked, then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"7233","6163 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi, Abu Amir al-Aqadi, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris, Sa'id ibn Mansur and Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us — the wording of Bishr ibn Umar — they said: Malik ibn Anas informed us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd Abu Ayyash who said: Sa'd ibn Malik was asked about white grain for sult. He said: Is there excess between them? I said: Yes. He said: Then no; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about fresh dates — returning to Bishr's wording — he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and said to those around him: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it."
"7234","6164 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Abu al-Mutarrif and Ibrahim, sons of Abi al-Wazir, narrated to us, they said: Malik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd Abu Ayyash, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was asked about fresh dates for dry dates, so he asked those with him: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it."
"7235","6165 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar and Abu Amir narrated to us, they said: Malik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyash, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about fresh dates for dry dates, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to those around him: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it."
"7236","6166 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyash, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and he said to those around him: Does it decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. He said: Then no."
"7237","6167 - And al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan who said: Abu Ayyash, freedman of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and said: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it. Thus Malik ibn Anas narrated this hadith with no disagreement among its narrators, nor additions, except in al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb's hadith saying freedman of Sa'd, which we found in no other's. As for Usamah ibn Zayd, Ibn Wahb narrated it from him as we mentioned in this chapter. And al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated it from him, differing in its chain."
"7238","6168 - As al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb ibn Hayyan al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Usamah ibn Zayd and another narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from one of the Prophet's Companions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and said: Does fresh decrease? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fresh is not sold for dry. So al-Layth ibn Sa'd and Ibn Wahb differed over Usamah in this hadith's chain. Then we considered whether another besides them narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Yazid."
"7239","6169 - So we found Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi, from Sa'd: that he was asked about two men trading sult for barley. Sa'd said: Two men traded in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dates and fresh dates, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does fresh decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it. Thus Ibn Uyaynah narrated it, and this is impossible; for Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi is an eminent Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abdullah ibn Yazid never met his like — he narrates from Abu Salamah and his peers. This is severe confusion, especially since al-Thawri narrated this hadith from Isma'il, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from an unnamed man, except Abu Hudhayfah named him."
"7240","6170 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of Ayyash, from Sa'd ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned it. This also increased its weakness and confusion, for this Ayyash we do not know. Then we considered whether another besides those we mentioned narrated it from Abdullah ibn Yazid."
"7241","6171 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Yazid: that Zayd Abu Ayyash informed him, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling dates for fresh dates on credit."
"7242","6172 - And we found Muhammad ibn Abdah ibn Abdullah al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Abu Tawbah al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Sallam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir who said: Abdullah informed me that Abu Ayyash informed him: that he heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling fresh dates for dry dates on credit. Yahya ibn Abi Kathir was unsurpassed in eminence among those who narrated this hadith from Abdullah ibn Yazid, so he established that the Prophet's prohibition therein was upon credit. In that is evidence of the corruption of its text along with the earlier corruption of its chains. Then we found this hadith narrated from a man whose clientage is to Banu Makhzum, unnamed, from whom Imran ibn Abi Anas narrated; and the one who narrated it from Imran ibn Abi Anas is not less than Yahya ibn Abi Kathir — namely Ibn al-Ashajj."
"7243","6173 - As Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him from Imran ibn Abi Anas: a freedman of Banu Makhzum narrated to him: that he asked Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas about a man advancing fresh dates for dry dates to a term. Sa'd said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us this. Bukayr said: And this we forbid. So clear, by praise of Allah and His favor, is the corruption of this hadith in its chain and text together, and that it is no proof against Abu Hanifah and his followers in opposing it. Analogy also requires it, for the sunnah permitted sale of fresh for fresh like for like, without regard to future equality or difference, indicating likewise fresh for dry dates when sold like for like, equal for equal, are permissible, without regard to the fresh's later decrease after drying from the dates sold for it. The sunnah also permitted dates for dates like for like, wheat for wheat like for like, barley for barley like for like — things known to change after sale by drying and decrease — yet that was not considered therein; rather their states on the day of sale were considered, nothing else. Added to the corruption of the basis the proponents clung to, cutting their proof and barring what they argued with whose corruption is clear as we mentioned. And success is from Allah."
"7244","1002 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding squatting in prayer — what is it? 6174 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahini narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade sitting on the side and squatting in prayer. It was not clarified what the forbidden squatting is. Scholars differed therein; a group — among them Abu Hanifah — says therein:"
"7245","What Ahmad ibn Abdullah al-Kufi narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Abu Yusuf who said: I said to Abu Hanifah: Do you dislike squatting in prayer? He said: Yes. That squatting with them is a man sitting on his heels in his prayer upon his buttocks. And they argued therein."
"7246","6175 - With what Bakkar narrated to us, Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us. 6176 - And with what Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, then they agreed, saying: from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ali, I love for you what I love for myself, and dislike for you what I dislike for myself; do not squat on your heels in prayer. And what I found in my book."
"7247","6177 - From Bahr ibn Nasr who said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade me to squat in my prayer like the wolf's squatting upon the heels. The meaning of upon the heels, with the authentication of the prior hadith, refers to Abu Hurayrah's heels, not the wolf's, for the wolf has no heels. In this hadith is evidence for what they said about the modality of the forbidden squatting. Abu Ubaydah mentioned that hadith scholars used to say therein: it is a man placing his buttocks on the ground raising his thighs. That was among what those holding that view argued."
"7248","6178 - With what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Abu Nadrah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said: While a shepherd grazed at al-Harrah, a wolf seized a sheep, and the shepherd came between the wolf and the sheep. So the wolf squatted on its tail and said to the shepherd: Do you not fear Allah Almighty? You come between me and provision Allah drove to me! The shepherd said: Wonder at a wolf squatting on its tail speaking to me in human speech! The wolf said to the shepherd: Shall I tell you of something more wondrous than me?! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between the two Harrahs narrates to people tidings of what passed. So the shepherd drove his sheep to Medina, gathered them in a corner, then entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him what the wolf said. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the people and said to the shepherd: Tell people what you saw. The shepherd stood narrating to people what the wolf said, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The shepherd spoke the truth. Indeed among the signs of the Hour is beasts speaking to men. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the Hour will not come until beasts speak to men, a man's sandal strap and whip lash speak to him, and his thigh informs him of what his family did after him."
"7249","6179 - And with what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from his father, from Tamim ibn Mahmud, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the crow's pecking, the beast's squatting, and a man fixing a place in the mosque as the camel fixes. So they inferred thereby that the squatting forbidden in prayer for humans is what they said therein. What these reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought about the modality of the mentioned squatting is the squatting of one forbidden it, so the praying one should not do either of them in his prayer. If an objector says: It was narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with them, that they used to squat in their prayers, then he mentioned."
